{"title":"1954 AHSME Problems/Problem 42","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_42\">Problem 42</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1954_AHSME_Problems/Problem_42&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 42\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider the graphs of \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/d/84deece22a8eb46f2ccd71a02b810b0bb22c4f32.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(1)\\qquad y=x^2-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\]\" width=\"183\" height=\"37\" /> \nand \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/2/ef280510c51f3df7b80e29c0cc506f4ec9a192ee.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(2)\\qquad y=x^2+\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\]\" width=\"183\" height=\"37\" /> \non the same set of axis. \nThese parabolas are exactly the same shape. Then: \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/e/81e238f0be1c6c03adcd3c68d6a8f342316a3ba9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\text{the graphs coincide.}\\\\ \\textbf{(B)}\\ \\text{the graph of (1) is lower than the graph of (2).}\\\\ \\textbf{(C)}\\ \\text{the graph of (1) is to the left of the graph of (2).}\\\\ \\textbf{(D)}\\ \\text{the graph of (1) is to the right of the graph of (2).}\\\\ \\textbf{(E)}\\ \\text{the graph of (1) is higher than the graph of (2).}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"400\" height=\"96\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1954_AHSME_Problems/Problem_42&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let us consider the vertices of the two parabolas. The x-coordinate of parabola 1 is given by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/8/d98ac4f893b6342707b17ef60a158dd94950c733.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{2} = \\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"50\" height=\"41\" />.\n</p><p>Similarly, the x-coordinate of parabola 2 is given by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/e/09ef52a6c28f93608ade5e247709170e8ae7d4d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\frac{-1}{2}}{2} = -\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"75\" height=\"41\" />.\n</p><p>From this information, we can deduce that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/8/8780ebc04febc9ea5ff4f50b23c8a197dd59e4b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\text{the graph of (1) is to the right of the graph of (2).}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"400\" height=\"17\" />, since the parabolas are the same shape.\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017161326\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.008 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.019 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 29/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 1531/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:18589:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017161326 and revision id 121730. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}